<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Taira no Kiyomori</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1118">1118</a>–1181</span> · <a href="/centuries/12th-century">12th century</a> · Also known as Kiyomori</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Taira no Kiyomori was a Japanese military leader and court noble of the late Heian period who, as Daijō-daijin, was the de facto ruler of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/japan">Japan</a> from 1167 until his death, and established the first <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/samurai">samurai</a>-dominated administrative government in Japanese history. Head of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/taira-clan">Taira clan</a> from 1153, he helped put down the Hōgen Rebellion in 1156 and defeated Minamoto no Yoshitomo in the Heiji Rebellion of 1160, but only exiled Yoshitomo's sons <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/minamoto-no-yoritomo">Yoritomo</a> and Yoshitsune. In 1167 he became the first courtier of a warrior family made chief minister; in 1179 he staged a coup, and in 1180 he put his grandson Antoku on the throne. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/genpei-war">Genpei War</a> began that year, and he died of sickness in 1181.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/taira-no-kiyomori.jpg" alt="Taira no Kiyomori" width="330" height="369" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Fujiwara no Gōshin · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Taira_no_Kiyomori,TenshiSekkanMiei.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>A noble out of favour could lose everything: when the warrior <b>Taira no Kiyomori</b> ruled after 1159, he removed and exiled 45 court officials.</blockquote><a href="/societies/heian-japan">Social Hierarchy of Heian Japan</a></li></ul></section>
